communicating-vessels-answer-list = { $ml } mL of oil: h_oil = { $oil }, h_water = { $water }
communicating-vessels-answer-why = A liquid in communicating vessels stays still when the pressure is the same at one level in both arms — otherwise it would flow to where it is lower. With water alone that means the same levels. At the level of the boundary the oil column presses on the left, the water column on the right: ρ_oil·g·h_oil = ρ_water·g·h_water. Oil is lighter than water, so its column has to be taller to press as hard.
communicating-vessels-answer-say = The oil column is taller — oil is lighter than water!
communicating-vessels-answer-say-2 = Let us look at the ruler.
communicating-vessels-answer-text = {" "}(a difference of { $value })
communicating-vessels-answer-text-2 = Water alone stood at one level in both arms{ $value }. Then: { $l }. The oil column is taller than the water column each time, and their ratio stays the same: the pressures are equal at the boundary, ρ_oil·h_oil = ρ_water·h_water. Hence the oil’s density ρ = ρ_water·h_water / h_oil ≈ { $rho } kg/m³ (the table gives { $rhoO } kg/m³).
